   "text": "# 2026-10-23: contract-definition server stall delays option snapshots ~20 min\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Date | Friday 2026-10-23, 10:35–11:00 ET (regular trading hours) |\n| Severity | Minor: data late, one index snapshot lost |\n| Detected by | The first hourly progress card (sent at 16:16 ET, when `progress_check` was first enabled), not at the time |\n| Status | Resolved; fixes merged (see Actions) |\n\n## Impact\n\n- **Option snapshots: no slot lost, but late.** The 10:35 slot failed, its retry succeeded at 10:42:17\n  (7 min late), and the next four slots started 3.5–5.5 min late until 11:00, when the schedule caught up.\n- **Index snapshots: the 10:35 slot was lost.** Both attempts (10:35:01 and 10:38:02) hit the 2-min task\n  timeout; the 10:40 slot ran at 10:45:29.\n- Equity bars (Alpaca) were not affected.\n- Late snapshots are still valid point-in-time data: each row is stamped with its real `ingested_at`.\n\n## Timeline (ET, from `pipeline_runs` and Airflow task logs)\n\n| Time | Event |\n|---|---|\n| 10:30:01 | Normal run: 3,364 option contracts in 213 s; indices in 5 s |\n| 10:35:01 | Option run starts. The first chain (`spx_monthly`) waits on its first request; after `RequestTimeout` (300 s) it raises `TimeoutError()` |\n| 10:35:01 | Index run starts, gets no answer, killed by the 2-min execution timeout at 10:37:01 |\n| 10:38:02 | Index retry: same, killed at 10:40:02 |\n| 10:41:16 | Option run ends: the 6-min run budget was used up, so `spx_weekly` failed and the other chains were skipped. Every chain failed, so the task failed |\n| 10:42:17 | Option retry (same 10:35 slot) succeeds: 3,060 contracts by 10:45:26 |\n| 10:45:29–10:56:47 | Slots 10:40, 10:45, 10:50, 10:55 each start when the previous run ends (`max_active_runs=1`), 3.5–5.5 min late |\n| 11:00:01 | Back on schedule |\n\nDuring the stall, the Gateway logged no data-farm errors and quotes kept flowing before and after it.\n\n## Root cause\n\n**IBKR's contract-definition (secdef) server stopped answering for about 5 minutes.** At the time, every run asked\nit for the underlying contract (`qualifyContracts`) and the listed expirations (`reqSecDefOptParams`) before any quote\nrequest, once per chain. The option and index tasks used separate API connections but stalled at the same time.\nWhat they had in common was these contract-definition requests, not market data. So one slow upstream service held up\na run whose actual job, streaming quotes, didn't need it.\n\n## Contributing factors\n\n- `RequestTimeout = 300 s` (sized for cold contract-detail downloads) let one hung request use the whole 6-min run budget.\n- Retrying a failed run pushes the following slots back while `max_active_runs=1`.\n- The RTH coverage metric bins runs by `started_at`. It counted the late 10:35 retry (started 10:42) as the\n  10:40 slot and reported the 10:35 slot as missing (77/78), although that snapshot exists.\n\n## Actions\n\n| Action | Commit | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cache underlyings, expirations and index contracts per New York day in the 08:15 `contract_cache` run; runs make no contract-definition requests | `c2dd697` | Done |\n| No retries for option and index snapshots (the next slot is the retry); one failure card per failing streak | `d0f3b5d` | Done. Trade-off: a failed run is now lost instead of late. On this day the retry kept the 10:35 option snapshot |\n| Coverage metric: count a run toward the slot in its `run_id`, not its start time | — | Open |\n\n## Lessons\n\n- Look at what a run actually depends on. The pipeline only needed quotes, but it depended on a second IBKR service\n  that it could just as well have asked once a day.\n- A timeout sized for the slowest legitimate request (a cold download) is too long for a request that is normally instant.\n"
